5.3 Layer 1 – Base OS Image

The Golden AMI process should begin with an approved and supported base operating-system image.

Supported Sources

The base image may originate from:

AWS-provided AMIs
AWS Marketplace images
Approved enterprise AMIs
Previously approved internal Golden AMIs
Base Image Requirements

The base image must:

Use a supported OS version.
Have a defined support lifecycle.
Be approved by the platform/security team.
Use the correct architecture.
Have unnecessary software minimized.
Have a documented source AMI ID.
Be tracked by OS version and image version.
Example
Windows Server 2022
        +
Approved Base AMI
        +
OS Version
        ↓
Image Builder Pipeline

The source AMI should be treated as an immutable input to the build process.

5.4 Layer 2 – Hardening Layer

The hardening layer establishes the organization's standard security configuration.

The hardening process should be automated rather than manually applied to individual EC2 instances.

Hardening Components
Operating System
CIS Level 1 baseline
Password policy
Account controls
File-system permissions
Security configuration
OS auditing
Network Security
Host firewall
Required ports only
Restricted administrative access
SSH hardening
RDP hardening
Service Hardening
Disable unnecessary services.
Remove unnecessary packages.
Restrict unnecessary listening ports.
Apply least-privilege service accounts.
Flow
Base OS
   ↓
CIS Baseline
   ↓
Security Configuration
   ↓
Firewall Configuration
   ↓
Service Hardening
   ↓
Hardened OS

Any approved exceptions should be documented rather than embedded as undocumented manual changes.

5.5 Layer 3 – Security Agent Layer

Required enterprise security agents should be installed as part of the image build where appropriate.

Examples include:

Endpoint protection agent
Vulnerability management agent
Security monitoring agent
Configuration/compliance agent
Other enterprise security tooling
Design Principle

The image should contain the approved agent software and baseline configuration, while environment-specific registration or identity should occur during deployment when necessary.

For example:

Golden AMI
    |
    +── Security Agent Package
    |
    +── Agent Configuration
    |
    +── Required Certificates
    |
    └── Deployment Registration
             |
             ▼
        EC2 Production

This prevents an AMI from being permanently associated with a specific production machine identity.

